---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def from '../calculators/carpet';
import { fmt } from '../lib/format';

const rooms: [number, number][] = [[10, 10], [10, 12], [12, 12], [12, 14], [12, 16], [14, 16], [15, 20]];
const table = rooms.map(([l, w]) => ({ l, w, a: l * w, yd: (l * w) / 9, yd10: (l * w * 1.1) / 9 }));
---

<CalcPage
  slug="carpet-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Carpet Calculator: Measure Square Feet & Yards"
  description="Measure carpet in square feet and yards, add rooms, and estimate roll length, padding, and cost. Choose 12, 13.5, or 15 ft rolls and your waste allowance."
  h1="Carpet Calculator"
  updated="2026-11-08"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Enter room measurements to estimate carpet in square feet and square yards, plus padding and length off a 12,
    13.5, or 15-foot roll. Add rooms to a project for a combined area estimate.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="measure">How to measure a room for carpet</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Measure the finished floor's length and width, including recesses that will receive carpet. Use feet and inches or metric units.</li>
    <li>For an L-shaped room, split the floor into rectangles that do not overlap. Add closets as separate measured sections.</li>
    <li>Enter each section and select <strong>Add to project</strong>. The calculator adds the areas before applying your waste percentage.</li>
    <li>Choose the product's roll width and check the proposed seams with the installer before ordering.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="how-to">How to calculate square footage for carpet</h2>
  <p>
    Multiply the room's length by its width for square feet, add about 10% for seams and trimming, then divide by 9.
    A <strong>12 × 14 ft room is 168 square feet, or about 20.5 square yards</strong> with 10% extra.
  </p>
  <p class="formula">square yards = length (ft) × width (ft) × 1.10 ÷ 9</p>
  <p>
    The 1.10 factor represents a chosen 10% allowance, not a fixed requirement. Enter your own waste percentage for
    the layout. A 12 × 14 ft room has 168 ft² of floor area; at 10% extra the estimate is 184.8 ft², or 20.53 yd²
    before display rounding. Carpet pad remains based on the original 168 ft².
  </p>

  <h2 id="table">Carpet for common room sizes</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Room</th><th class="num">Sq ft</th><th class="num">Sq yd (exact)</th><th class="num">Sq yd with 10%</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{table.map((r) => <tr><td>{r.l} × {r.w} ft</td><td class="num">{r.a}</td><td class="num">{fmt(r.yd, 1)}</td><td class="num">{fmt(r.yd10, 1)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="roll-width">Why roll width matters</h2>
  <p>
    Broadloom carpet comes in rolls, most often 12 feet wide, sometimes 13.5 or 15 feet. A room 12 feet wide or less can
    be covered by one piece cut to length. Wider rooms need a seam, and the installer may order extra so the pile runs the
    same direction on both sides. That is why an installer's measure can come in higher than a simple area calculation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Square feet, square yards, and linear feet measure different things.</strong> Using the same 184.8 ft²
    purchase estimate, divide by 9 for 20.53 square yards, or by a 12 ft roll width for 15.4 linear feet. A linear foot
    is a one-foot-long cut across the full roll width; it is not one square foot.
  </p>
  <p>
    The roll-length result is an area-based allowance, not a cutting plan. For example, a 10 × 10 ft room cut as one
    unseamed piece from a 12 ft roll needs a piece at least 10 ft long: 120 ft² before trimming. Dividing the room's
    100 ft² by 12 gives only 8.33 ft, which cannot cover that room in one piece. Matching patterns, pile direction,
    and offcuts between rooms can change the final order even when a waste percentage is included.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pad">How much carpet pad do you need?</h2>
  <p>
    Pad is cut to fit the room and seamed without regard to direction, so you need about the room's area with little
    waste. The calculator shows it separately.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tiling the bathroom or entry instead? The <a href="/tile-calculator/">tile calculator</a> counts tiles by size and grout joint.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>How do I convert square feet to square yards?</h3>
  <p>Divide by 9, since a square yard is 3 × 3 feet. 180 square feet is 20 square yards.</p>
  <h3>Should I measure closets and stairs?</h3>
  <p>
    Add closets as separate rectangles. Measure each stair's tread, riser, and width, and ask the installer to include
    the nosing, fitting method, and pile direction in a stair cutting plan. This area calculator does not lay out stair
    pieces or determine an installer's per-step charge.
  </p>
  <p>
    Comparing with hard flooring? Try the <a href="/flooring-calculator/">flooring calculator</a> or the
    <a href="/square-footage-calculator/">square footage calculator</a> for irregular rooms.
  </p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Carpet = area × (1 + waste %) ÷ 9 for square yards.</li>
    <li>Roll length = carpet square feet ÷ roll width, assuming pieces can be seamed.</li>
    <li>Pad = room area ÷ 9, with no waste added.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
